// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  04 · LEFT JOIN — keeping the unmatched rows                   ★★☆ core
//  concepts: LEFT JOIN · unmatched rows · NULL in results
//  run: node 04-left-join.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  INNER JOIN answers "which orders, and who placed them". It cannot
//  answer "how is every customer doing", because a customer with no orders
//  has nothing to match and so vanishes from the result entirely.
//
//  LEFT JOIN is the fix: keep every row of the LEFT table, and when the
//  right side has no match, fill its columns with NULL.
//
//  Build the same report twice, both ordered by customer id then order id:
//
//    · onlyCustomersWhoOrdered(db)   customers INNER JOIN orders
//    · everyCustomerWithOrders(db)   customers LEFT  JOIN orders
//
//  Both rows are shaped { customer, order_id, total }. The left version
//  has extra rows, and in those the right-hand columns are empty:
//
//      { customer: 'Edsger', order_id: null, total: null }
//
//  hint: NULL arrives in JavaScript as `null`, not `undefined` — the key
//  is present, the value is empty.

import { test, eq, ok } from '../../_lib/check.js';
import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';

// ── provided: the same little shop in every file of this module ──────────
//   8 customers · 6 products · 12 orders · 20 line items
//   money is in CENTS (integers — floats and money do not mix)
//   Edsger and Katherine have never ordered; nobody ever bought the
//   laptop stand. Those three gaps are what the joins are for.
