// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  07 · GROUP BY over a join, and the COUNT trap — SOLUTION   ★★★ stretch
//  run: node 07-group-by-with-joins.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Walkthrough: GROUP BY splits the joined rows into buckets and returns
//  one row per bucket. Group by `c.id`, not `c.name` — two customers can
//  share a name, ids cannot.
//
//      SELECT c.name                          AS customer,
//             COUNT(o.id)                     AS orders,
//             COALESCE(SUM(o.total_cents), 0) AS revenue,
//             AVG(o.total_cents)              AS avg_order
//        FROM customers c
//        LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id
//       GROUP BY c.id
//       ORDER BY c.name
//
//  Row-wise: Ada's three joined rows collapse into one, and the aggregates
//  summarise the three. Edsger has ONE joined row — the LEFT JOIN's
//  padding, with every orders column NULL — and it collapses into one row
//  too. That single padded row is the whole trap.
//
//      COUNT(*)      counts rows           → Edsger: 1   ← wrong
//      COUNT(o.id)   skips NULLs           → Edsger: 0   ← right
//
//  The aggregates split three ways on empty input, and all three are worth
//  memorising: COUNT gives 0, SUM gives NULL, AVG gives NULL. So SUM needs
//  COALESCE(…, 0) to be a number your JSON consumer can add up, while AVG
//  is honestly null — the average of nothing is not zero, and writing 0
//  there would drag your dashboard's mean down with imaginary customers.
//  Rule of thumb: over a LEFT JOIN, never COUNT(*). Count the joined
//  table's primary key, which is NULL exactly when nothing matched.
//  In app code: every "customers, with order count" admin table.

import { test, eq, ok, approx } from '../../_lib/check.js';
import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';

// ── provided: the same little shop in every file of this module ──────────
