// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  11 · subqueries — a query used as a value — SOLUTION       ★★★ stretch
//  run: node 11-subqueries.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Walkthrough: two kinds of subquery, both here.
//  A DERIVED TABLE sits in FROM and is treated like a table that exists
//  only for this statement. It is how you aggregate an aggregate — SQL
//  will not let you write `AVG(SUM(x))`, so you total per customer in the
//  inner query and average those totals in the outer one:
//
//      SELECT AVG(spend) AS average
//        FROM (SELECT SUM(o.total_cents) AS spend
//                FROM orders o
//               GROUP BY o.customer_id)
//
//  A SCALAR SUBQUERY is one that yields a single value and is used as one.
//  Drop the same query into HAVING and every group is compared against the
//  whole table's summary:
//
//      HAVING SUM(o.total_cents) > (SELECT AVG(spend) FROM (…))
//
//  Notice who is in the denominator. The inner query starts FROM orders,
//  so Edsger and Katherine — who have none — are not averaged in. Start it
//  FROM customers with a LEFT JOIN instead and the average drops by a
//  quarter and four names become five. Neither is wrong; they answer
//  different questions, and you have to know which one you meant.
//  The bar sits at 27883.33 and Ada is at 28200 — 1.1% above. Round the
//  average to an integer "for tidiness" and she is still in, but a nudge
//  either way and the report changes without the code changing. Money in
//  cents as integers, comparisons unrounded, formatting only at the edge.
//  In app code: "accounts above the plan average", "posts above the median
//  score", and — when the subquery would be re-run per row — the reason
//  window functions exist.

