// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  13 · indexes on the columns you join on                    ★★★ stretch
//  concepts: EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN · CREATE INDEX · SCAN vs SEARCH
//  run: node 13-join-indexes.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  A join is a lookup, and a lookup with no index is a full table scan.
//  The planner will tell you which one it is doing — you never have to
//  guess, and you should never assume.
//
//    · planFor(db, sql, ...params)  → the planner's own words for that
//                                     query, as one string
//    · addJoinIndexes(db)           → create an index on orders(customer_id)
//                                     and one on order_items(order_id),
//                                     returning their names in that order
//
//  `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN <sql>` returns rows with a `detail` column. Join
//  the details with ' | ' and hand back the string.
//
//      SCAN o                                    ← reads every row
//      SEARCH o USING COVERING INDEX idx_… (…)   ← jumps straight to them
//
//  Make creation idempotent so it is safe to run on every startup.
//
//  hint: sqlite indexes `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` for free — the side that
//  needs help is the one holding the foreign key.

